/// Chapter 37 of the guide, written from md-docs/learn/17-pdf-and-slides.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page PdfAndSlidesGuide(path: "/docs/guide/pdf-and-slides", title: "PDF and slides", description: "Make PDF documents and slide decks: the paged engine, CSS layout, page breaks, fonts, Arabic, headers, page numbers, contents, charts and QR codes.", layout: DocsShell("PDF and slides", kind: .tutorial, chapter: t("nav.group.outputs"), blurb: "Compile the same language to a paginated PDF document or a slide deck — invoices, reports and talks, laid out with CSS as on the web.", source: "md-docs/learn/17-pdf-and-slides.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "choose-what-the-build-writes", label: "Choose what the build writes"), Anchor(id: "the-paged-engine", label: "The paged engine"), Anchor(id: "make-a-pdf-document", label: "Make a PDF document"), Anchor(id: "make-a-slide-deck", label: "Make a slide deck"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/desktop", prevLabel: t("ch.36"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/server-rendering", nextLabel: t("ch.38"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to compile a WebFluent project to paper: a paginated PDF document, such as an invoice or a report, or a slide deck for a talk. You write the pages with the same elements and the same CSS as a website, and `wf build` lays them out on pages.\n\nIn this chapter:\n\n- choosing what the build writes;\n- how the paged engine lays out a page;\n- making a PDF document: paper, headers, page breaks, contents, charts, fonts and right-to-left text;\n- making a slide deck.")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("You need a WebFluent project. To start one already set up for paper, run one of these commands:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf init my-report --template pdf"),
                Line(command: "wf init my-deck --template slides")
            ])

        Markdown("Then run `wf build` in the project's folder. The build prints the page count and writes one file, `build/<name>.pdf`, named after the project unless `output_filename` says otherwise:")

        CodeBlock(#"  PDF: 45586 bytes, 3 page(s)
  Output: ./build/my-report.pdf
Build complete."#, title: "text")

        Markdown("To produce one PDF per invoice from data, use the same templates with [server rendering](/docs/guide/server-rendering) instead.")

        Heading("Choose what the build writes", id: "choose-what-the-build-writes").h2

        Markdown("`build.output_type` in `webfluent.app.json` chooses what `wf build` writes:")

        Table(caption: "Choose what the build writes") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Output").header
                    Table.Cell.header { Markdown("`output_type`") }
                    Table.Cell("Writes").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A website")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`spa` (default), with `build.ssg` for pre-rendered pages; or `static`, which is the same as `ssg`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("HTML, CSS and JavaScript ([Static sites and SPAs](/docs/guide/static-and-spa))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("PDF document")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`pdf`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("One `.pdf`, paginated") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Slide deck")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`slides`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("One `.pdf`, one slide per page") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Custom elements")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`elements`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`elements.js` for other frameworks ([JavaScript interop](/docs/guide/interop#publish-your-components-as-custom-elements))") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("The paged engine", id: "the-paged-engine").h2

        Markdown("A PDF is the page, printed. The compiler renders the program to the same static HTML and CSS a template renders, then lays that HTML out on paper itself, with no browser and no external tool. The engine covers five areas:\n\n- **The page's own stylesheet.** The engine's rules for every built-in, the theme's tokens, every `.css` file under `src/` and every `style \{ \}` block go through a real CSS cascade. It supports selectors with descendant, child and sibling combinators, attribute selectors, `:first-child`/`:nth-child`, `:not()`, `:is()` and `:where()`; specificity; inheritance; `var()`; and `em`, `rem` and `%`. A `Card` in a PDF looks like the `Card` on the page, and a class of your own (`class: \"callout\"`) brings its rules with it. `@media print` applies; rules only a screen has (`:hover`, a media query on width) do not.\n- **Layout.** Block, flex and grid work as CSS defines them — `Row`, `Stack`, `Grid` and `Column` sit side by side exactly as on the web — with absolute positioning, `gap`, `min-`/`max-` sizes, `aspect-ratio`, inline-blocks on a line of text (a badge in a sentence), tables sized by their content, and list markers.\n- **Text.** Every run is shaped (ligatures, kerning, Arabic joining, combining marks), set right to left where the language is, broken at Unicode's line-break opportunities, and aligned or justified. A word too long for its line is cut rather than overflowing.\n- **Paint.** Backgrounds (colours, linear and radial gradients with any number of stops, pictures), borders per side (solid, dashed, dotted) with rounded corners, shadows, opacity, `overflow: hidden`, `transform: rotate()`, and pictures with their transparency — PNG, JPEG (embedded as it is), WebP, GIF, and SVG, which stays vector.\n- **The file.** Fonts are embedded as subsets with the text attached, so a reader can copy and search it. The file is compressed, carries its title and author, has an outline of its headings in the reader's sidebar, and its links work.")

        Heading("Make a PDF document", id: "make-a-pdf-document").h2

        Markdown("To build a PDF, set `output_type` to `pdf`. The `pdf` key sets the paper, margins (in points) and default font for every document in the project:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "build": {
    "output_type": "pdf",
    "pdf": {
      "page_size": "A4",
      "margins": { "top": 72, "bottom": 72, "left": 72, "right": 72 },
      "default_font": "Helvetica",
      "default_font_size": 12,
      "output_filename": "report.pdf"
    }
  }
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("A PDF page holds a `Document`, and everything inside it flows from page to page. This invoice has a header on every page but the first, a page number in the footer, a watermark, a table, and a totals card that is never split:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Line { item: String, qty: Number, price: Number }

const LINES: [Line] = [
    Line(item: "Design", qty: 12, price: 90),
    Line(item: "Build", qty: 40, price: 110),
]

page Invoice(path: "/", title: "Invoice", description: "An invoice.") {
    derived total = LINES.map(l => l.qty * l.price).sum()
    Document(size: "A4", title: "Invoice 2026-014", author: "Acme Studio") {
        Header(on: .rest) { Text("Acme Studio · invoice 2026-014").muted.sm.right }
        Footer { Text("Page {page} of {pages}").muted.sm.center }
        Watermark("PAID")

        Row(justify: .between, align: .start) {
            Heading("Invoice").h1
            Stack(gap: .xs) {
                Text("No. 2026-014").bold
                Text("Issued {format("2026-09-01", .date, "long")}").muted
            }
        }
        Table {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row { Table.Cell("Item")  Table.Cell("Qty")  Table.Cell("Price")  Table.Cell("Amount") }
            }
            Table.Body {
                for l in LINES {
                    Table.Row {
                        Table.Cell(l.item)
                        Table.Cell("{l.qty}")
                        Table.Cell(format(l.price, .currency))
                        Table.Cell(format(l.qty * l.price, .currency))
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        Card {
            style { break-inside: avoid; margin-top: 12pt }
            Text("Total: {format(total, .currency)}").bold.lg
        }
        PageBreak
        Heading("Terms").h2
        Paragraph { Text("Payment within 30 days.") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The result is a two-page PDF: the invoice on the first page, ending with `Total: $5,480.00`, and the terms on the second, under the header.")

        Heading("Set the paper, headers and footers", id: "set-the-paper-headers-and-footers").h3

        Markdown("These elements shape every page of a document:")

        Table(caption: "Set the paper, headers and footers") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Element").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Document(size:, landscape, margin:, title:, author:, subject:, keywords:, lang:)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The paper (`A4`, `A3`, `A5`, `Letter`, `Legal`, `Tabloid`, or two lengths: `\"210mm 297mm\"`), its margins (one length or four, `\"20mm 25mm\"`), and what the file says about itself. What it leaves out, `build.pdf` decides") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Header`, `Footer`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Drawn in the top and bottom margins of every page, or of the pages `on:` names: `.all` (the default), `.first`, `.rest`, `.odd`, `.even`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Background`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Drawn behind each page's content, the size of the whole page, margins and all: a cover's full-bleed gradient, a coloured band, a sidebar strip that runs down every page. It takes `on:` too") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Watermark(\"DRAFT\")`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Large, faint, rotated text behind each page. Its look is the rule `.wf-watermark`, which a stylesheet may change") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`PageBreak`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Starts a new page; so do `break-before: page` and `break-after: page` in a style") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("Inside a `Header`, `Footer` or `Background`, `page` and `pages` are the page's number and the page count: `Text(\"Page \{page\} of \{pages\}\")`.\n\nThe page's background — the `html` or `body` rule's — fills every page, edge to edge, so a dark theme prints dark.")

        Heading("Control where pages break", id: "control-where-pages-break").h3

        Markdown("Content flows onto as many pages as it needs, and never breaks through the middle of a line of text. The engine follows these rules:\n\n- Paragraphs break between lines, keeping at least two on each side (`orphans`, `widows`).\n- Tables break between rows, and **the header row is drawn again** at the top of every page the table continues on.\n- A grid breaks between its rows; a row of cards moves to the next page together.\n- Side-by-side columns taller than a page break each on its own, and what follows waits for the longer column.\n- A heading never ends a page: it moves with what follows.\n- A box that runs onto the next page is painted on both, its border open at the break.\n\nTo keep a box whole — a totals block, a signature — give it `break-inside: avoid`, as the invoice's totals card does. To start a new page, place `PageBreak`.")

        Heading("Add a table of contents, links and an outline", id: "add-a-table-of-contents-links-and-an-outline").h3

        Markdown("`TableOfContents(levels: 2)` lists the document's headings with the page each one lands on, dotted leaders between them, and each entry a link to its heading. The engine lays the document out, learns where every heading landed, and fills in the numbers. `title:` puts a heading above the list.\n\nA `Link` is a link in the PDF too: to a URL, or `to: \"#terms\"` to the element whose `id:` is `terms`. Every heading appears in the reader's outline, nested by level.")

        Heading("Draw charts and QR codes", id: "draw-charts-and-qr-codes").h3

        Markdown("This report draws a grouped bar chart, a donut chart and a QR code from a constant list of records:")

        CodeBlock(#"const SALES = [
    { month: "Jan", web: 41, store: 22 },
    { month: "Feb", web: 48, store: 25 },
    { month: "Mar", web: 55, store: 21 },
]

page Report(path: "/", title: "Sales", description: "The quarter.") {
    Document {
        Heading("Sales by channel").h1
        Chart(kind: .bar, data: SALES, x: "month", y: ["web", "store"], unit: "k")
        Chart(kind: .donut, data: SALES, x: "month", y: "web", labels: true)
        QrCode("https://example.com/report/q1")
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`Chart` draws a list of records as vector graphics. It takes these props:")

        Table(caption: "Draw charts and QR codes") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Prop").header
                    Table.Cell("What it sets").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`kind`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.bar` (grouped, or `stacked`), `.line`, `.area`, `.pie` or `.donut`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`x`") }
                    Table.Cell("The field that names each row's label")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`y`") }
                    Table.Cell("The field that holds each row's value, or a list of fields (one series each)")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`colors`, `ink`") }
                    Table.Cell("The series' colours, and the colour of the labels")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`grid`, `legend`") }
                    Table.Cell("Whether to draw grid lines and a legend")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`labels`") }
                    Table.Cell("Values written on bars and slices")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`unit`") }
                    Table.Cell("A unit after each value")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`width`, `height`") }
                    Table.Cell("The size in user units. The chart scales to its box, so draw a narrow chart narrower for larger text")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("`QrCode(value, color:, background:)` encodes a URL or any text.\n\nBoth work on a web page too, as an SVG drawn when the page is built. There, what they read must be known at build time — a literal, a `const` or a `data` file — or the build reports `E109`.")

        Heading("Use your own fonts", id: "use-your-own-fonts").h3

        Markdown("The default faces are Liberation Sans, Serif and Mono — the metric twins of Helvetica, Times and Courier — built into `wf`. So `Helvetica`, `Arial`, `system-ui`, `sans-serif`, `Times`, `serif`, `Courier` and `monospace` all resolve with nothing installed, and cover Latin, Greek and Cyrillic.\n\nTo use any other family, add its file:\n\n1. Put `.ttf` or `.otf` files under the project's `fonts/` folder (or `src/fonts/`, or name them in `pdf.fonts`).\n2. Name the family as the file names it, for example in the theme: `font-family: Manrope, sans-serif`.\n\nVariable fonts are drawn at the weight a style asks for. A weight or an italic a family does not have is synthesised. A font a stylesheet's `@font-face` names, from a local file, is used too.\n\nA character no named face has is drawn from a face that has it — the project's fonts first, then the machine's. The build names, in a `note:` line, each family it took from the machine, so you can make the document the same everywhere by copying those fonts into `fonts/`. To forbid the machine's fonts, set `\"system_fonts\": false`. A character no font has is reported, never drawn as `?`.")

        Heading("Lay out a right-to-left document", id: "lay-out-a-right-to-left-document").h3

        Markdown("A document whose `lang` is right to left (`ar`, `he`, `fa`, `ur`) is laid out right to left — text, flex rows, grids, table columns, list markers. A run of the other direction inside it is ordered by the Unicode bidirectional algorithm.\n\nA code or a figure written in Latin characters inside Arabic text — an invoice number like `2026-0417`, a product code — can come out with its parts in the wrong order. The bidirectional algorithm reads the hyphen as part of the right-to-left text around it. To fix it, give the code a run of its own that says it is left to right: `Text(\"2026-0417\", class: \"ltr\")`, with `.ltr \{ direction: ltr; unicode-bidi: isolate; \}` in the project's CSS. The fix works on a web page and in a PDF alike.")

        Heading("What a PDF does not draw", id: "what-a-pdf-does-not-draw").h3

        Markdown("Everything static draws. What only means something in a browser is refused where it is written, with `E109`:\n\n- controls (`Button`, `Input`, `Form`, …);\n- navigation (`Router`, `Navbar`, `Tabs`, `Menu`);\n- overlays (`Modal`, `Dialog`, `Toast`, `Tooltip`);\n- media that plays (`Video`, `Audio`, `Carousel`);\n- elements a script draws (`Host`, `Element`, `Portal`);\n- handlers, `resource` and motion.")

        Heading("Make a slide deck", id: "make-a-slide-deck").h2

        Markdown("To build a deck, set `output_type` to `slides`. The `slides` key sets the deck's size and its chrome:")

        CodeBlock(##"{
  "build": {
    "output_type": "slides",
    "slides": {
      "size": "16:9",
      "show_slide_numbers": true,
      "footer_text": "Acme — Q4 review",
      "background_color": "#FFFFFF"
    }
  }
}"##, title: "json")

        Markdown("A deck is a `Presentation` inside a page. This deck uses every kind of slide, and restyles the last one with a gradient:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Deck(path: "/", title: "Q4 review", description: "The quarter in slides.") {
    Presentation {
        TitleSlide("Q4 review", subtitle: "Acme Studio, October 2026")
        SectionSlide("Numbers").primary
        Slide {
            Heading("Revenue").h2
            List {
                List.Item { Text("Up 18% on Q3") }
                List.Item { Text("Three new enterprise accounts") }
                List.Item { Text("Churn at 1.2%") }
            }
        }
        TwoColumn {
            Container {
                Heading("What worked").h3
                Text("Self-serve onboarding.")
            }
            Container {
                Heading("What did not").h3
                Text("The March pricing test.")
            }
        }
        ImageSlide(src: "chart.png", caption: "Monthly recurring revenue")
        Slide {
            style { background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1E3A8A, #0F172A); color: #fff }
            Heading("Thank you").h1
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The result is a six-page PDF, one slide per page.")

        Heading("Write the slides", id: "write-the-slides").h3

        Markdown("A deck is laid out by the same engine, one slide to a page. A slide is a box the size of the page, with `slides.margin` as its padding (a rule of the deck's own overrides it). What runs past a slide's edge is clipped, with a note naming the slide.\n\nThe slide kinds:")

        Table(caption: "Write the slides") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Element").header
                    Table.Cell("What it draws").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Slide \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("A freeform slide, laid out as a column from the top")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`TitleSlide(title, subtitle:)`") }
                    Table.Cell("A cover, centred")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`SectionSlide(label)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A full-bleed band; `.primary`, `.success`, `.danger`, `.warning` or `.info`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`TwoColumn \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("Two equal columns")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`ImageSlide(src:, caption:)`") }
                    Table.Cell("A picture with an optional caption")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("Each kind is styled by the engine's sheet, and a project's rules or a `style \{ \}` block change it as anywhere. Everything a document draws, a slide draws: layout, charts, pictures, tables, Arabic.\n\nA `Presentation` holds slides, a `for` or an `if` over them, and components whose body is a slide. So a deck writes its section openers, headings and footers once, and makes a slide per item of its data. A page's `derived` values are worked out before the deck is drawn, so a slide can show a figure computed from a `data` file.")

        Heading("Size and frame the deck", id: "size-and-frame-the-deck").h3

        Markdown("These keys under `build.slides` set the deck's size and chrome:")

        Table(caption: "Size and frame the deck") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Key").header
                    Table.Cell("What it sets").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`size`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`16:9` (default, 960×540pt), `4:3` (720×540), `A4-landscape` (841.89×595.28), or `WIDTHxHEIGHT` in points") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`width`, `height`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Override `size` outright") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`show_slide_numbers`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Draws `n / total` in the bottom right") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`footer_text`") }
                    Table.Cell("Text in the bottom left")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`chrome_color`") }
                    Table.Cell("The colour of the numbers and footer; when unset, it flips between dark and light on each slide's background")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`background_color`") }
                    Table.Cell("Paints every slide")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`default_font`, `default_font_size`") }
                    Table.Cell("The deck's font, and its size (24pt). Every size in the engine's slide rules is relative to it")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`output_filename`") }
                    Table.Cell("The file the build writes")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Table(caption: "Troubleshooting") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("What you see").header
                    Table.Cell("What to do").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`E109` on a `Button`, a handler or a `resource`") }
                    Table.Cell("Remove it from the document or deck: a PDF has nothing to run it")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`E109` on a `Chart` or `QrCode` in a web page") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Give it data known at build time: a literal, a `const` or a `data` file") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `note:` names a family taken from the machine") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Copy that font into `fonts/` so the document is the same on every machine") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A note says a slide was clipped")
                    Table.Cell("Shorten the slide, or split it in two")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A totals block or signature is split across two pages")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Add `break-inside: avoid` to its `style \{ \}`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A Latin code in Arabic text shows its parts out of order")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Give it a left-to-right run (see [Lay out a right-to-left document](#lay-out-a-right-to-left-document))") }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
